## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of the open/closed principle, polymorphism, and runtime extensibility. It also checks the ability to add new parsing backends without touching the core importer.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern
- Registry/Plugin registry
- Open-Closed principle
- Dependency injection

## Code Example
```javascript
class Parser {
  parse(input) {
    throw new Error('not implemented');
  }
}

class CSVParser extends Parser {
  parse(input) {
    // CSV parsing logic
    return [];
  }
}

class YAMLParser extends Parser {
  parse(input) {
    // YAML parsing logic
    return [];
  }
}

class ParserRegistry {
  constructor() {
    this.parsers = new Map();
  }

  register(extension, parser) {
    this.parsers.set(extension, parser);
  }

  get(extension) {
    return this.parsers.get(extension);
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new ParserRegistry();
registry.register('.csv', new CSVParser());
registry.register('.yaml', new YAMLParser());

// Core importer (unchanged)
function importFile(filePath) {
  const ext = path.extname(filePath);
  const parser = registry.get(ext);
  return parser.parse(fs.readFileSync(filePath));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime-extensible backends using patterns beyond a single factory, with practical concerns like data transfer and policy-based selection.

## Key Concepts
- **Strategy**: Choose a backend at runtime based on plan characteristics
- **Registry**: Register backends by name and instantiate lazily
- **Abstraction**: Small Backend interface minimizes ground-truth coupling
- **Safety**: Backends declare capability and isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
interface Plan { /* ... */ }
interface ExecContext { /* ... */ }
interface ExecutionResult { rows: any[]; metadata?: Record<string, unknown>; }

interface Backend {
  supports(plan: Plan): boolean;
  execute(plan: Plan, ctx: ExecContext): Promise<ExecutionResult>;
}

type BackendFactory = () => Backend;

class BackendRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, BackendFactory>();
  private instances = new Map<string, Backend>();

  register(name: string, factory: BackendFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  get(name: string): Backend | null {
    if (!this.instances.has(name)) {
      const factory = this.factories.get(name);
      if (!factory) return null;
      this.instances.set(name, factory());
    }
    return this.instances.get(name)!;
  }

  listAvailable(): string[] {
    return Array.from(this.factories.keys());
  }
}

class QueryPlanner {
  constructor(private registry: BackendRegistry) {}

  private selectBackend(plan: Plan): Backend | null {
    const available = this.registry.listAvailable();

    // Policy: prefer GPU for vectorizable plans, fallback to CPU
    if (available.includes('gpu')) {
      const gpu = this.registry.get('gpu');
      if (gpu?.supports(plan)) return gpu;
    }

    if (available.includes('fpga')) {
      const fpga = this.registry.get('fpga');
      if (fpga?.supports(plan)) return fpga;
    }

    // Default fallback
    if (available.includes('cpu')) {
      return this.registry.get('cpu');
    }

    return null;
  }

  async execute(plan: Plan, ctx: ExecContext): Promise<ExecutionResult> {
    const backend = this.selectBackend(plan);
    if (!backend) throw new Error('No suitable backend found');
    return backend.execute(plan, ctx);
  }
}

// Usage example
const registry = new BackendRegistry();

// Register backends at runtime
registry.register('cpu', () => new CPUBackend());
registry.register('gpu', () => new GPUBackend());
registry.register('fpga', () => new FPGABackend());

const planner = new QueryPlanner(registry);

const plan: Plan = { type: 'vector_scan', data: [1,2,3,4] };
const ctx: ExecContext = { sessionId: 'abc123' };

// Planner picks optimal backend based on policy
const result = await planner.execute(plan, ctx);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design runtime extensibility for security-critical components, including hot-reload and safe concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime pluggability via a policy registry
- Evaluation order and conflict resolution
- Thread-safety and atomic swaps for hot-reload
- RBAC/ABAC/PBAC composition and performance trade-offs

## Code Example
```java
interface Policy {
  boolean evaluate(User user, Resource resource, Action action);
  String name();
}
class PolicyRegistry {
  private final java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference<java.util.Map<String, Policy>> policies =
      new java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicReference<>(java.util.Collections.emptyMap());

  public void register(String name, Policy p) {
    var next = new java.util.HashMap<>(policies.get());
    next.put(name, p);
    policies.set(java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(next));
  }

  public boolean evaluate(User user, Resource resource, Action action) {
    for (Policy p : policies.get().values()) {
      if (p.evaluate(user, resource, action)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  public void reload(java.util.Map<String, Policy> newPolicies) {
    policies.set(java.util.Collections.unmodifiableMap(new java.util.HashMap<>(newPolicies)));
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you determine policy precedence when multiple policies apply with conflicting results?
- How would you test hot-reload paths without impacting live requests?

## Why This Is Asked
