## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design extensible, runtime-pluggable components with clear interfaces and minimal core coupling.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy/Plugin pattern
- Runtime registry
- Composition
- Dynamic loading
- Type-safe interfaces

## Code Example
```typescript
// Core interfaces and types
type Event = { source: string; payload: any }

interface Enricher {
  enrich(e: Event): Event
}

class Registry {
  private enrichers = new Map<string, Enricher[]>()

  register(sourceType: string, enricher: Enricher): void {
    if (!this.enrichers.has(sourceType)) {
      this.enrichers.set(sourceType, [])
    }
    this.enrichers.get(sourceType)!.push(enricher)
  }

  get(sourceType: string): Enricher[] {
    return this.enrichers.get(sourceType) || []
  }
}

// Core pipeline
class Pipeline {
  constructor(private registry: Registry) {}

  process(event: Event): Event {
    const enrichers = this.registry.get(event.source)
    return enrichers.reduce((e, enricher) => enricher.enrich(e), event)
  }
}

// Usage example
class GeoEnricher implements Enricher {
  enrich(event: Event): Event {
    // Add geo data based on event payload
    return { ...event, geo: { lat: 40.7, lon: -74.0 } }
  }
}

class AnomalyDetector implements Enricher {
  enrich(event: Event): Event {
    // Add anomaly detection flag
    return { ...event, anomaly: false }
  }
}

const registry = new Registry()
registry.register('sensorA', new GeoEnricher())
registry.register('sensorA', new AnomalyDetector())

const pipeline = new Pipeline(registry)
const result = pipeline.process({ source: 'sensorA', payload: { temp: 25 } })
```

This design enables:
- Runtime registration of new enrichers
- Type-safe composition
- Source-agnostic core pipeline
- Easy extensibility without modifying core logic

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a runtime-extensible command processing system while balancing dynamic plugin loading with reliability and isolation. It surfaces trade-offs between discoverability, versioning, and failure handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Command pattern** with a pluggable registry for runtime flexibility
- **Runtime registration** of handlers enabling plugin-based architecture
- **Idempotency guarantees** to prevent duplicate processing
- **Isolated execution** to ensure fault containment between commands
- **Retry policy** with exponential backoff for transient failures
- **Testing implications** for dynamic behavior and handler contracts

## Code Example

```typescript
interface Command {
  name: string;
  payload: unknown;
  idempotencyKey?: string;
}

interface CommandHandler<T = unknown> {
  handles: string;
  execute(payload: T): Promise<void>;
}

class CommandDispatcher {
  private handlers = new Map<string, CommandHandler>();

  register(handler: CommandHandler): void {
    this.handlers.set(handler.handles, handler);
  }

  async dispatch(command: Command): Promise<void> {
    const handler = this.handlers.get(command.name);
    if (!handler) throw new Error(`Unknown command: ${command.name}`);
    await handler.execute(command.payload);
  }
}
```

## Trade-offs

| Concern | Trade-off |
|---------|-----------|
| Runtime flexibility | Adds complexity; handlers must implement strict contracts |
| Isolation | Worker processes increase memory overhead |
| Idempotency | Requires persistent storage; may limit throughput |
| Discovery | Central registry can become a bottleneck at scale |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to plug in new algorithms without touching the orchestrator, a common interview scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for interchangeable thresholds
- Thin interface and per-customer configuration
